P=[
('The Library That Never Closes','reading','A town library began leaving a small collection of books in a weatherproof box outside its entrance. People could borrow books at any hour without a library card. At first, the staff worried that books would disappear. Instead, most visitors returned them and many brought books of their own. After three months, the library noticed that young people who had never entered the building were using the box. The staff then invited these visitors to help choose new titles. The project did not replace the library; it helped more residents discover it.',[
('Why was the book box placed outside?',['To let people borrow books at any time','To sell used books','To store damaged books','To replace the library'],0),('What had the staff been concerned about?',['The weather would be too hot','Books might not be returned','The building was too small','Nobody could read the signs'],1),('What happened instead?',['People returned and donated books','The library stopped lending books','The box stayed empty','Young people damaged books'],0),('Which group began using the box?',['Only teachers','Young people new to the library','Only library staff','Tourists from abroad'],1),('What did the staff ask visitors to do?',['Repair the roof','Pay a fee','Choose new titles','Change the opening hours'],2),('What does “it” in the final sentence refer to?',['The box','The library','The staff','The project'],1),('What is the main idea?',['Small changes can make libraries more accessible','Libraries should close early','All books should be free online','Young people dislike reading'],0),('Which statement is supported?',['The project attracted new visitors','The library stopped using cards','Visitors could only borrow on weekends','The box was inside the building'],0)]),
('The Phone-Free Lunch','reading','At Westhill School, students started a voluntary phone-free lunch once a week. A group of students designed the idea after noticing that many friends sat together but rarely spoke. Participants placed their phones in their bags for twenty minutes and played simple conversation games. In the first week, only twelve students joined. By the sixth week, more than eighty were taking part. Some students said lunch felt less stressful, while others missed checking messages. The school did not ban phones. Instead, organizers encouraged everyone to decide whether taking a short break from screens was useful for them.',[
('Why did students introduce the activity?',['They wanted more face-to-face conversation','Phones had been stolen','Teachers required a new rule','Lunch was too short'],0),('How long did participants put phones away?',['Five minutes','Twenty minutes','An hour','All day'],1),('Was the activity compulsory?',['Yes, for every student','Only for teachers','No, it was voluntary','Only for new pupils'],2),('How did participation change?',['It fell to twelve','It stayed exactly the same','It grew substantially','It ended after a week'],2),('What did some participants report?',['Lunch felt less stressful','They stopped attending school','Games were banned','They lost their phones'],0),('What was one different reaction?',['Some wanted longer school days','Some missed their messages','Some asked for more homework','Some refused to eat'],1),('What is the school’s approach?',['Permanent restrictions','Encouraging personal choice','Punishing students who use phones','Replacing lunch with games'],1),('What best describes the organizers’ goal?',['Help students experiment with screen breaks','Teach phone repair','Sell new phones','Remove all school rules'],0)]),
('A Garden on the Roof','reading','When an apartment building in a crowded city installed a rooftop garden, residents expected it to look attractive. They were surprised by other benefits. The plants provided shade, and the rooms directly below the roof became slightly cooler during the summer. Neighbors who had hardly spoken before began meeting to water the plants. The garden also attracted insects that helped pollinate nearby flowers. However, the project required regular maintenance and careful checks to make sure the roof could support the extra weight. The residents now advise other buildings to consult an engineer before starting a similar project.',[
('What was the original expectation?',['The building would look nicer','The residents would earn money','A restaurant would open','The apartments would get larger'],0),('What happened to rooms below the roof?',['They became a little cooler','They became darker in winter','They became noisier','They were converted to shops'],0),('What social benefit is mentioned?',['Neighbors started meeting','More people moved out','Residents stopped sharing spaces','Fewer families visited'],0),('Why were some insects useful?',['They reduced rent','They pollinated flowers','They painted the roof','They cleaned the water'],1),('What is a challenge?',['The garden needs maintenance','No one is allowed on the roof','Plants cannot grow in cities','The building has no stairs'],0),('What should buildings check first?',['Whether the roof can bear the weight','Whether tourists will visit','Whether every resident owns a car','Whether there is a school nearby'],0),('What does “similar” refer to?',['A rooftop-garden project','A new apartment purchase','A public road','A new heating system'],0),('What is the author’s purpose?',['Describe benefits and practical concerns','Warn everyone never to grow plants','Advertise one gardening company','Explain how to build a roof'],0)]),
('The Lost Wallet Experiment','reading','For a social science project, Maya placed twenty empty wallets in different public locations. Each wallet contained a note explaining the experiment and an email address. She wanted to find out whether people would contact her after finding one. Within a week, thirteen people had written to her. Several described where they found the wallets, and one person suggested adding a phone number for people who rarely use email. Maya understood that her results could not represent an entire country because the experiment involved only one town and a small sample. She published the findings together with these limitations.',[
('What was Maya investigating?',['Whether finders would contact her','How much wallets cost','Where to buy leather','Which town had more shops'],0),('What was inside each wallet?',['Cash and a card','A note and an email address','A photograph and money','A key and a ticket'],1),('How many people contacted Maya?',['Seven','Ten','Thirteen','Twenty'],2),('What did one finder suggest?',['Adding a phone number','Changing the town','Using only red wallets','Hiding the note'],0),('Why were the conclusions limited?',['Small sample from one town','All wallets contained money','The email was broken','The wallets were expensive'],0),('What did Maya do when publishing?',['Hid the limitations','Explained the limitations','Claimed to study the entire world','Destroyed her notes'],1),('What is a “sample” in this context?',['A small group used for research','A shop selling wallets','A type of email','A school exam'],0),('What does the experiment suggest?',['Some people respond to a request to return a found item','Every person returns wallets','Email never works','All cities behave alike'],0)]),
('The Repair Café','reading','Once a month, volunteers in a small town invite residents to bring broken household objects to a community center. Instead of immediately throwing away a lamp or a toaster, visitors can learn how to repair it. The volunteers do not promise that every object can be fixed. They explain safety concerns and sometimes recommend buying a replacement. The café has become popular with teenagers who want practical experience. A local business donated tools, while residents contribute spare parts. Organizers measure success not only by the number of repaired objects but also by the skills people learn.',[
('What can visitors bring?',['Broken household objects','Only new computers','Only books','Food for sale'],0),('What is different from an ordinary repair shop?',['Visitors can learn during repairs','Every repair is guaranteed','Only staff can enter','Repairs happen daily'],0),('What do volunteers sometimes recommend?',['Buying a replacement','Ignoring safety','Throwing away all objects','Paying for a new course'],0),('Who gained practical experience?',['Teenagers','Only engineers','Only small children','Tourists'],0),('What did a business provide?',['Tools','Free bicycles','A new building','Uniforms'],0),('What do residents provide?',['Spare parts','New phones','Exams','Bus tickets'],0),('How is success measured?',['Repairs and new skills','Only money earned','Only number of visitors','Only social media followers'],0),('What is the main message?',['Repair projects can teach useful skills','Broken objects are always dangerous','Young people dislike practical tasks','Repairing is always cheaper'],0)]),
('A City That Listens','reading','A city council created an online map where residents could report places that were difficult to use. People marked broken sidewalks, missing ramps and crossings where traffic lights changed too quickly. The council expected mostly complaints, but many residents also offered practical suggestions. Staff reviewed the reports and gave each one a public status, such as received, under review or completed. After six months, the city had repaired several busy crossings. The council emphasized that the map was only one way to share concerns and kept a telephone service for residents without internet access.',[
('What could residents report?',['Accessibility problems in public places','Only missing pets','School exam results','Private shopping lists'],0),('What surprised the council?',['Residents offered solutions too','Nobody used the map','All sidewalks were perfect','People refused to report'],0),('What did each report receive?',['A public status','A prize','A school grade','A new phone'],0),('What was repaired after six months?',['Several busy crossings','Every building','All parks','The city hall roof'],0),('Why keep the telephone service?',['To include people without internet','To advertise new devices','To reduce public access','To replace the map entirely'],0),('Which problem appears in the text?',['Missing ramps','Expensive textbooks','High electricity bills','Long school holidays'],0),('What can be inferred about the system?',['It helps track progress on reports','It guarantees every report is solved instantly','It keeps reports secret','It accepts no suggestions'],0),('What is the main idea?',['Citizen reports can guide practical improvements','People should avoid online maps','Cities should close crossings','The council only wants complaints'],0)])]
V=[('Research and Evidence',[('evidence','facts that support a conclusion'),('reliable','able to be trusted'),('assume','believe without proof'),('reveal','make information known'),('conclude','reach a judgment'),('investigate','examine carefully'),('significant','important or large enough to matter'),('accurate','correct and without errors')]),('Education and Learning',[('achieve','succeed in reaching a goal'),('improve','make something better'),('challenge','a difficult task or situation'),('require','need as necessary'),('participate','take part in an activity'),('knowledge','information and understanding'),('independent','not controlled by another person'),('strategy','a plan for reaching a goal')]),('Environment and Society',[('reduce','make smaller in amount'),('protect','keep safe from harm'),('environment','the natural world around us'),('resource','something useful that is available'),('community','people living or working together'),('prevent','stop something from happening'),('benefit','a helpful or positive result'),('contribute','give something to help achieve a result')]),('Digital Life',[('privacy','control over personal information'),('access','ability to reach or use something'),('device','a piece of equipment'),('available','ready to be used'),('frequently','often'),('efficient','working well without wasting time'),('respond','answer or react'),('impact','a strong effect')]),('Decisions and Change',[('consider','think carefully about'),('convince','make someone believe or agree'),('alternative','another possible choice'),('eventually','in the end after some time'),('opportunity','a chance to do something'),('avoid','stay away from or prevent'),('likely','probably going to happen'),('purpose','reason for doing something')]),('Work and Responsibility',[('responsible','having a duty to do something'),('volunteer','work without being paid'),('schedule','a plan of events and times'),('maintain','keep in good condition'),('encourage','give support or confidence'),('solution','an answer to a problem'),('necessary','needed to achieve something'),('effective','producing the intended result')])]

def default_activities():
 out=[]
 for i,(title,kind,passage,qs) in enumerate(P):
  out.append({'slug':f'reading-{i+1:02}','title':title,'kind':'reading','duration':25,'text':passage,'questions':[{'prompt':q,'options':opts,'answer':ans} for q,opts,ans in qs]})
 for i,(title,pairs) in enumerate(V):
  pool=[y for x,y in pairs]
  qs=[]
  for j,(word,definition) in enumerate(pairs):
   distract=[pool[(j+3)%len(pool)],pool[(j+5)%len(pool)],pool[(j+6)%len(pool)]]
   options=[definition,*distract];shift=(j+i)%4; options=options[shift:]+options[:shift]
   qs.append({'prompt':'Choose the correct meaning of: '+word,'options':options,'answer':options.index(definition)})
  out.append({'slug':f'vocabulary-{i+1:02}','title':'Vocabulary Quest: '+title,'kind':'vocabulary','duration':12,'text':'Choose the most accurate English definition for each word.','questions':qs})
 # Six combined exams with unique passages and vocab, 16 questions, use exact percentages through weighted points
 for i in range(6):
  passage=P[i][2]; q=[{'prompt':p,'options':o,'answer':a} for p,o,a in P[i][3]]
  words=V[i][1]; definitions=[x[1] for x in words]
  for j,(w,d) in enumerate(words):
   options=[d,definitions[(j+2)%8],definitions[(j+4)%8],definitions[(j+5)%8]]
   shift=(i+j)%4;options=options[shift:]+options[:shift]
   q.append({'prompt':'Vocabulary: '+w,'options':options,'answer':options.index(d)})
  out.append({'slug':f'simulation-{i+1:02}','title':'Module E Simulation '+str(i+1)+' – '+P[i][0],'kind':'simulation','duration':75,'text':passage,'questions':q})
 return out
